module Core

### Represents an opaque native pointer for FFI / runtime-integration use.
### `CPtr` is the type-erased pointer wrapper used at the C boundary — equivalent to
### `void*` in C. It carries no information about what it points at; users responsible
### for ensuring the pointer is used consistently with the foreign side's expectations.
###
### `CPtr` replaces the per-handle wrappers that v0.0.1a previously used for arbitrary-
### precision runtime integration (formerly `BigIntHandle` / `BigDecHandle`). Per-handle
### type-distinctness was discarded in favor of one canonical opaque pointer; the runtime
### still distinguishes which native object each handle refers to internally.
###
### :see: {Hijacked[T]} for the typed raw-pointer concept used in RF-internal plumbing.
@llvm("ptr")
record CPtr obeys Assignable
  pass

### Returns the raw address stored in this pointer.
### This is a low-level runtime integration surface, not a stable user API.
### :returns: The resulting `Address` value.
@readonly
routine CPtr.get_address() -> Address
  return LLVM::pointer_to_int[CPtr, Address](me)

### Returns a bitwise duplicate of this raw pointer.
### `CPtr` is `@llvm("ptr")` so it does not auto-derive `Assignable`; this trivial
### opt-in body makes `=` and `with` legal at the cost of one line. Aliasing is
### the user's problem — the same FFI contract that lets `CPtr` exist at all.
### :returns: A bitwise duplicate of this pointer.
@readonly
routine CPtr.assign() -> CPtr
  return me

### Reinterprets a raw address as `CPtr`.
### This does not validate ownership or liveness of the pointed runtime object.
### :param from: The address to wrap.
### :returns: The resulting `CPtr` value.
dangerous routine CPtr(from: Address) -> CPtr
  return LLVM::reinterpret_bits[Hijacked[CPtr], CPtr](LLVM::int_to_pointer[Address, CPtr](from))

### Returns the null `CPtr` value.
### This sentinel indicates that no live runtime object is associated with the pointer.
### :returns: The resulting `CPtr` value.
routine cptr_none() -> CPtr
  return CPtr(0_addr)

### Returns `true` when this pointer is the null sentinel.
### :returns: The resulting `Bool` value.
@readonly
routine CPtr.is_none() -> Bool
  return LLVM::int_eq(me, cptr_none())

### Formats this pointer as its raw address.
### :returns: The resulting `Text` value.
@[readonly, override]
routine CPtr.represent() -> Text
  danger
    return me.get_address().represent()

### Diagnostic representation for debugging — same as `represent`.
### :returns: The resulting `Text` value.
@[readonly, override]
routine CPtr.diagnose() -> Text
  return "CPtr(" + me.represent() + ")"

### Compatibility helper: alias of `cptr_none()` for legacy bigint-handle call sites.
### Deprecated — prefer `cptr_none()`.
routine bigint_handle_none() -> CPtr
  return cptr_none()

### Compatibility helper: alias of `cptr_none()` for legacy bigdec-handle call sites.
### Deprecated — prefer `cptr_none()`.
routine bigdec_handle_none() -> CPtr
  return cptr_none()

